      <floats-group id="article-20132.s15">
        <fig id="article-20132.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>The Facial Nerve. This figure depicts the plan of the facial and intermediate nerves and their communication with other nerves. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=gray+plate+788&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray788" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-20132.s16">
        <fig id="article-20132.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Facial and Trigeminal Nerve Interactions in Lacrimal Gland Function. This illustration shows the facial and trigeminal branches contributing to lacrimal gland function and includes the maxillary, great superficial petrosal, facial, deep petrosal, and Vidian nerves. Other labeled nerves include the anterior and middle alveolar, posterior dental, orbital, glossopharyngeal, and oculomotor nerves,&#x000a0;as well as the alveolar plexus. Ganglia in this section include the sphenopalatine and superior cervical ganglia. Blood vessels include the internal carotid artery and internal jugular vein. Henry Vandyke Carter, Public Domain, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray779" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-20132.s17">
        <fig id="article-20132.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Parasympathetic Innervation of the Lacrimal Gland.&#x000a0;The parasympathetic supply to the lacrimal gland originates from the superior salivary nucleus. Fibers travel through the nervus intermedius to the geniculate ganglion before joining the greater superficial petrosal nerve. These fibers then merge with the deep petrosal nerve to form the Vidian nerve (nerve of the pterygoid canal), which carries them to the pterygopalatine ganglion, where they synapse. Postganglionic fibers then follow various nerves, including the zygomaticotemporal and lacrimal nerves, to reach and innervate the lacrimal gland. Contributed by Koushik Tripathy, MD&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="lacrimal__gland__supply" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
